ing to continue  to do  what they do and i'm not sure how you  expanded any further i mean they  said you have to interview more minority  candidates okay they did that and more  minorities were interviewed than ever  before that part of it worked and then  they were going to give these incentives  to teams that developed minority  uh candidates who were hired well they  they've done that now so i don't know  where you go from here how you expand  the rule because ultimately it is up to  these owners to to hire minority  candidates and they just haven't done it  year after year after year after year  and and here be enemy sits yet again  looking looking for a head coaching job  that hasn't come to him and frankly like  you said he's earned it he's the next  guy he's in the batter's box and he's  been in the batter's box now for three  years  one of the other aspects of the hiring  process that others have advocated we've  been banging that drum this hiring cycle  as well  tabling all interviews and hires until  after the super bowl roger goodell was  asked about that today here's that  exchange  this is um an issue we had a lot of  discussion with the fritz pilot alliance  who we worked very closely on developing  the policies this offseason  many coaches  and you know it's something i'm sure  will be brought back up again  as you know this off this season uh as  part of our policy where technology  really actually was a benefit for us  was this technology we're going through  right now virtual they were able to have  interviews  by zoom and that gave coaches an  opportunity to interview without being  as disruptive for their preparations  while they're in the postseason head  coaches felt better about it i've talked  to a couple other coaches that are in  the league now that didn't get an  opportunity both white and black who  talked about that potential change  and so we'll have to balance that and  think if if that's the right  outcome or whether there's some other  alternative  they've gotten a phone call from  microsoft after the gratuitous reference  to zoom that's a different business  issue altogether for the national  football league i probably shouldn't  have said that but i don't care um and  the point is yeah they're going to talk  about it you know they talk about it  they talk about everything let's talk  about this let's talk about that there's  a difference between talk and action  there's plenty of time for talking and  they talk and they talk and they talk  and they talk they're so afraid of  unintended consequences that becomes the  excuse for avoiding any meaningful  change and i hate that phrase unintended  consequences to me sharing is a cop-out  for we don't want to or we're not able  to sit down and brainstorm the  reasonably foreseeable consequences of  what we are going to do it doesn't take  a whole lot of creativity or brain power  to spot 10 15 20 things that you can  anticipate on the back end of any major  change you make and yes there would be  unintended consequences but no  unforeseen no first there would be  expected consequences that you would  weigh as part of the effort to consider  this change but i think it's a smart  change i think it's a necessary change  because what it does it eliminates that  inherent conflict of interest that the  assistant coach has regardless of race  regardless of background regardless of  anything when you're under consideration  for that job you're balancing how much  time do i put into preparation for my  interview how much time do i put into  getting ready for the next game in the  post season it's a horrible burden to  put on an assistant coach and the best  thing to do is table everything until  after the super bowl  and they're always worried mike about  falling behind in the race for assistant  coaches because if if somebody fills a  job early they're hiring the best  available assistant coaches and leaving  the rest out there for the last hire in  this 